                                                      E-COMMERCE & CUSTOMER ANALYTICS
                                            PostgreSQL + Power BI | Professional Project Documentation
1. Project Overview
An end-to-end E-Commerce & Customer Analytics project using PostgreSQL and Power BI.
The project transforms transactional data into business insights covering sales, customers, products, payments, order status and geographic performance.

2. Business Objectives
•	Measure revenue and order performance.
•	Understand customer purchasing behavior.
•	Identify repeat and high-value customers.
•	Perform RFM analysis and customer segmentation.
•	Identify top products and categories.
•	Monitor cancellations and returns.
•	Compare payment methods and cities.
•	Build interactive, executive-ready Power BI dashboards.

3. Technology Stack
•	PostgreSQL — data storage, cleaning and SQL analysis.
•	Power BI — dashboards, visualization and DAX.
•	Excel/CSV — source data and data inspection.
•	GitHub — portfolio and version control.

4. Dataset
|-----------------|--------------------------------------------|--------------------------- | 
|Table	          |          Purpose	                         |      KeyField              |
|-----------------|--------------------------------------------|----------------------------|
|customers	      |      Customer master information   	       |  customer_id               |
|orders	          |    Order transactions and status	         |  order_id                  |
|order_items	    |      Products, quantity and price per order|	 order_id / product_id    |
|products	        |    Product and category information        |	  product_id              |
|payments         |    	Payment information	                   |    order_id                |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
•	Orders: 50,000 records.
•	Customers table: 30,000 rows and 10,000 distinct customer IDs.
•	Customers with orders/order-items in joined analysis: 9,932.
•	Total revenue across all order statuses: ₹4,676,040,548.55.
•	Delivered: 39,081; Shipped: 4,925; Cancelled: 2,978; Returned: 3,016.
•	Order dates initially required order_date::date for date calculations.

5. Data Cleaning & Quality Checks
•	NULL customer IDs and important fields.
•	Duplicate customer IDs.
•	Order-status distribution.
•	Invalid or future dates.
•	Quantity <= 0.
•	Unit price <= 0.
•	Orphan foreign keys.
•	Column data types using information_schema.

Modeling note: the customers table contains duplicate customer_id values. For a production Power BI model, a one-row-per-customer dimension is recommended.

6. Business Questions / SQL Analysis

Analysis	                                                                                    Purpose
Month-over-Month Revenue Growth                                       Compare monthly revenue using window functions.
Top 10 Products by Quantity Sold                                      Rank products using SUM(quantity).
Top 10 Products by Revenue                                            Rank products using quantity × unit_price.
Category Performance                                                  Compare revenue by category.
Top Customers	                                                        Rank customers by order count.
Repeated Customers                                                    Identify customers with more than one order.
Customer Segmentation	                                                Classify customers as Low, Medium or High by revenue.
RFM Analysis	                                                        Calculate Recency, Frequency, Monetary and RFM scores.
Cancellation Analysis	                                                Calculate cancelled orders and cancellation rate.
Return Rate	                                                          Calculate returned orders and return rate.
Payment Method Analysis	                                              Compare orders, revenue and revenue contribution.
Geographic Analysis                                                  	Compare customers, orders and revenue by city.
Inactive Customers	                                                  Find customers with no purchase in 90+ days.
Data Cleaning	                                                        Validate data quality.
Project KPIs	                                                        Create core business metrics.

7. RFM Analysis
RFM means:
•	Recency — days since the customer's latest purchase; fewer days is better.
•	Frequency — number of orders; more orders is better.
•	Monetary — total customer spending; higher spending is better.

Each dimension was scored from 1 to 5 using NTILE(5). RFM Score = Recency Score + Frequency Score + Monetary Score.
Portfolio segmentation used: ≥12 High Value; 8–11 Medium Value; <8 Low Value.

